(doc
  (title "Architecture Decision Records — YX Protocol")
  (source-format markdown)
  (converted "2026-07-19")
  (kv "System" "YX Protocol")
  (kv "Version" "0.1.0")
  (kv "Last Updated" "2026-04-11")
  (para "This document records all significant architectural decisions made for the YX protocol. Each ADR captures the context, the decision, and the rationale.")
  (section
    (heading "D01 — UDP as Transport Layer")
    (kv "Status" "Accepted")
    (kv "Date" "2026-01-18")
    (subsection
      (heading "Context")
      (para "YX needs a transport layer for distributed system communication. Options considered: TCP, UDP, Unix sockets, named pipes."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Decision")
      (para "Use UDP exclusively. The protocol does not use TCP."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Rationale")
      (item "UDP provides connectionless, low-latency delivery suitable for real-time distributed systems")
      (item "The YX packet format (HMAC + GUID + payload) is self-contained; no connection state is needed")
      (item "Broadcast support is native to UDP — required for peer discovery")
      (item "Applications requiring reliability build it above the transport layer"))
    (subsection
      (heading "Consequences")
      (para "Implementations must handle packet loss at the application layer. No TCP fallback is defined.")))
  (section
    (heading "D02 — HMAC-SHA256 Truncated to 16 Bytes")
    (kv "Status" "Accepted")
    (kv "Date" "2026-01-18")
    (subsection
      (heading "Context")
      (para "Every packet requires authentication to prevent tampering. Full HMAC-SHA256 output is 32 bytes. Choices: 32 bytes, 16 bytes, shorter."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Decision")
      (para "Compute HMAC-SHA256 over (GUID || PAYLOAD) and take the first 16 bytes."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Rationale")
      (item "16 bytes (128 bits) provides 2^64 security against forgery (birthday bound)")
      (item "Reduces packet overhead vs 32-byte HMAC")
      (item "SHA-256 remains the strongest widely-deployed hash function; truncation is the standard approach (used in TLS, NIST SP 800-107)")
      (item "All implementations must use the same truncation; no configurable length"))
    (subsection
      (heading "Consequences")
      (para "The HMAC field is always exactly 16 bytes. Implementations must not use the full 32-byte output for comparison.")))
  (section
    (heading "D03 — Payload-Agnostic Transport Layer")
    (kv "Status" "Accepted")
    (kv "Date" "2026-01-18")
    (subsection
      (heading "Context")
      (para "Should the transport layer understand or validate payload content?"))
    (subsection
      (heading "Decision")
      (para "The transport layer treats payload as opaque bytes. It does not parse, validate, or interpret payload content."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Rationale")
      (item "Enables multiplexing any protocol over the transport layer")
      (item "Simplifies the transport implementation")
      (item "Protocol 0 and Protocol 1 are defined at a layer above transport")
      (item "A compromised key allows forged payloads — transport-layer payload validation provides no additional security"))
    (subsection
      (heading "Consequences")
      (para "Applications that use the transport directly must implement their own payload validation. The Protocol 0/1 layers provide structured payload handling.")))
  (section
    (heading "D04 — 6-Byte GUID (Not UUID)")
    (kv "Status" "Accepted")
    (kv "Date" "2026-01-18")
    (subsection
      (heading "Context")
      (para "Packets need unique identifiers for replay detection and correlation. Standard UUID (RFC 4122) is 16 bytes. Options: 4, 6, 8, 16 bytes."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Decision")
      (para "Use 6-byte (48-bit) cryptographically-random GUIDs."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Rationale")
      (item "6 bytes provides 2^48 ≈ 281 trillion unique values")
      (item "Collision probability negligible for any realistic deployment")
      (item "10 bytes smaller than UUID — significant savings at high packet rates")
      (item "Cryptographic randomness ensures unpredictability for replay window matching")
      (item "Simpler than UUID: no version bits, no variant bits, no timestamp encoding"))
    (subsection
      (heading "Consequences")
      (para "GUIDs are not globally unique across all time and space (unlike UUID v4) but are practically unique for YX's use case. Do not use GUIDs as globally-unique identifiers outside of a YX session.")))
  (section
    (heading "D05 — Python as Reference Implementation / Canonical Artifact Generator")
    (kv "Status" "Accepted")
    (kv "Date" "2026-01-18")
    (subsection
      (heading "Context")
      (para "Multiple language implementations must be wire-format compatible. A common test vector format is needed. Which language generates the canonical artifacts?"))
    (subsection
      (heading "Decision")
      (para "Python is the reference implementation. It generates canonical test vectors and reference packets in `canonical/` that all other implementations validate against."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Rationale")
      (item "Python implementation was first; it defines the correct wire format by construction")
      (item "Python is widely readable and auditable")
      (item "Canonical artifacts are language-agnostic JSON/binary — not Python-specific")
      (item "Future implementations (Rust, Go, etc.) can validate without a Python runtime by loading pre-generated JSON"))
    (subsection
      (heading "Consequences")
      (para "The Python implementation must be built and its canonical artifacts generated before any other implementation begins. Python implementation bugs that affect canonical artifacts require regenerating all artifacts and re-validating all implementations.")))
  (section
    (heading "D06 — Three-Layer Architecture (Transport / Protocol 0 / Protocol 1)")
    (kv "Status" "Accepted")
    (kv "Date" "2026-01-18")
    (subsection
      (heading "Context")
      (para "Applications need different payload semantics: some want JSON/RPC-style text messages, others want binary efficiency with compression and encryption."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Decision")
      (para "Define three layers: Transport (mandatory), Protocol 0 Text (optional), Protocol 1 Binary (optional). A 1-byte protocol identifier in the payload routes packets to the correct handler."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Rationale")
      (item "Clean separation: raw transport for applications that want full control, structured layers for common patterns")
      (item "The protocol byte in the payload is part of the payload spec, not the packet spec — transport layer remains truly agnostic")
      (item "Extensible: Protocol 2+ can be added without changing the wire format"))
    (subsection
      (heading "Consequences")
      (para "Implementations must implement the transport layer. Protocol 0 and Protocol 1 are defined as separate, optional handlers. All three are required for full feature parity per D04 (P4 — Multi-Language Parity).")))
  (section
    (heading "D07 — Replay Protection via TTL-Bounded Seen-GUID Store")
    (kv "Status" "Accepted")
    (kv "Date" "2026-01-18")
    (subsection
      (heading "Context")
      (para "Attackers can replay captured valid packets. Detection mechanism needed."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Decision")
      (para "Maintain an in-memory set of recently-seen GUIDs with a 300-second TTL. Packets with a GUID seen within the TTL window are rejected."))
    (subsection
      (heading "Rationale")
      (item "Simple and effective against replay attacks")
      (item "300-second window is long enough to handle clock skew and network delays")
      (item "In-memory store is sufficient; persistence across restarts is not required (replays across restarts require a fresh HMAC key anyway)")
      (item "TTL cleanup prevents unbounded memory growth"))
    (subsection
      (heading "Consequences")
      (para "GUIDs must be cryptographically random (D04) to prevent GUID prediction attacks. The TTL is normative (300 seconds) — implementations must not use a shorter window.")))
  (section
    (heading "D08: Mesh keys live in the macOS Keychain, never in repos (2026-07-19)")
    (para "**Decision:** HMAC mesh keys are stored in the login Keychain (`service org.spy.yx`, one account per mesh). Tools resolve: `--key` flag → `YX_KEY` env → Keychain → built-in dev key WITH loud warning. **Why:** the key IS mesh membership; the dev key is in public git history. **Consequence:** `yxkey` CLI (Swift) + `MeshKey` resolver in Primitives; python mirrors via `security` subprocess. Spec: key-management.sxp."))
  (section
    (heading "D09: Swift is the base implementation; Python is validation + edges (2026-07-19)")
    (para "**Decision:** New YX code (node daemon, tools, services) is written in Swift, consumed via SPM (this repo is the package). The Python implementation is kept for wire-format parity validation and for the two Python-bound edges (ib_async bridge, MLX tooling) until they migrate. No pip distribution. **Why:** the surrounding ecosystem (murphy, dagsmith, agent, memory, mind) is Swift; single codesigned binaries beat interpreter/venv maintenance."))
  (section
    (heading "D10: Discovery is pluggable — UDP broadcast now, Bonjour later (2026-07-19)")
    (para "**Decision:** Peer discovery v1 stays 255.255.255.255 broadcast (flat LAN). The node daemon treats discovery as a strategy so mDNS/Bonjour (NWBrowser / NetService) can be added without protocol changes. Discovery is NOT membership; the HMAC key remains the only trust boundary (D08)."))
  (section
    (heading "D11: S-expression is the canonical structured payload format (2026-07-19)")
    (para "**Status:** Accepted. **Author:** Scott D. Yelich.")
    (para "**Context.** yx Protocol 0 was defined as \"text / JSON-RPC\"; the node/message layer exchanged JSON objects (`{\"method\":…,\"params\":…}`) and briefly spooled messages as UNF (YAML-frontmatter + markdown). The surrounding ecosystem is s-expression native (ybs `.sbs`, `.sxp`, the portfolio data block, the FSMB message schema). JSON/JSONL repeatedly forces newline framing and full-document buffering to know when a value is complete.")
    (para "**Decision — across the board.** Any payload richer than plaintext is an **S-expression, not JSON.** S-expression is the base/default structured format, and is **preferred even over plaintext when practical.** Preference order for a payload: **S-expression > plaintext > binary** — use the most structured form that fits; drop to plaintext only for opaque pass-through text, binary only for non-text.")
    (para "Concretely for yx:")
    (item "The message body is a single s-expression: `(msg …)` (see message-format spec).")
    (item "Protocol 0's RPC/message layer migrates from JSON-RPC to s-expression forms **dispatched on the head symbol (car)**: `(node-hello …)`, `(node-info)`, `(msg …)`. The head atom is the verb; the rest are its arguments. No `{\"method\",\"params\"}` envelope.")
    (item "On-disk representations are `.sxp` s-expressions — never YAML/JSON/markdown wrappers. **UNF is retired.**")
    (para "**Rationale.**")
    (item "**Always-known parse state.** Track paren depth; a form is complete exactly when depth returns to 0. Framing is self-delimiting and uniform — no length prefix, no JSONL convention, no \"read to a terminator you may not have received yet.\" Critical for a datagram protocol and for streaming agent output (vs. the JSONL buffering murphy needs).")
    (item "**One grammar, uniform nesting** — structured data nests natively without escaping.")
    (item "**Ecosystem consistency** — everything else already speaks s-expr; JSON was the outlier.")
    (item "**Human-writable, no library required** — legible and hand-editable (FSMB-proven).")
    (para "**Consequences.**")
    (item "Protocol 0 is redefined: text = s-expression, dispatched on the car. Existing JSON-RPC handlers (`node.hello`/`node.info`/`msg.deliver`) get rewritten to s-expr forms; a transition shim MAY accept both briefly.")
    (item "`yxnode` stops writing UNF; it carries and (if spooling) stores `(msg …)` as `.sxp`.")
    (item "**JSON is confined to EXTERNAL edges** where a foreign contract requires it — the OpenAI-compatible LLM endpoints (mlx-router), MCP, third-party HTTP APIs. **Internal yx/agent payloads are never JSON.**")
    (item "Forward-looking and where-feasible: not a mandate to rewrite every existing JSON emitter at once (e.g. murphy's JSONL event stream is an external-ish contract — migrate when practical). New code follows this default from the start.")
    (item "Composes with D08: optional `(key-id …)`/`(sig …)` fields slot into the grammar later for per-message signing without a schema break."))
  (section
    (heading "D12: Membership ≠ trust — Ed25519-signed authority over a flat trust store (2026-07-19)")
    (para "**Status:** Accepted. **Author:** Scott D. Yelich. **Context:** the mesh will include untrusted machines (e.g. a family member's malware-prone PC). Such a node can hold the shared mesh key (membership) yet must NOT be able to make trusted nodes execute anything.")
    (para "**Decision — two tiers, cleanly separated:**")
    (item "**Membership** (join, send/receive chat, be seen) = the shared **HMAC mesh key** (+ optional AES). Anyone on the bus has it. Membership proves \"a mesh member sent an unmodified packet\" — it does NOT prove which member. Encrypted ≠ trusted.")
    (item "**Authority** (a message that gets ACTED ON — run a command, place an order, anything consequential) = a valid **Ed25519 signature** whose public key is in the receiver's **flat trusted-signers store**. Chat is accepted from any member; authority is honored ONLY from a trusted signer.")
    (para "**Rules:**")
    (item "Enforce at the point of action: the executor of a consequential action verifies `(sig)` over the canonical message bytes against a `(key-id)` in its trusted-signers set BEFORE acting. Never trust the `from` field; `from` is a claim, the signature is truth.")
    (item "Unsigned or untrusted-signed authority messages are received but REFUSED (logged; may be answered with a `reject`). Informational messages need no signature.")
    (item "**Flat trust store, not PKI.** No CAs/X.509/chains. A set of trusted Ed25519 pubkeys (SSH `authorized_keys` style), distributed via the vault or a committed config; private signing keys in the Keychain beside the mesh key (D08). Revoke = remove the pubkey; eject a whole node = rotate the mesh key.")
    (item "**Signing substrate:** the canonical S-expression form (D11) — Rivest csexp is built for signing; JSON canonicalization (JCS) is a known pain. `(msg …)` canonicalizes deterministically; sign those bytes with Ed25519.")
    (para "**Confidentiality caveat:** shared-key AES hides traffic from non-members, NOT from members — every mesh member (incl. an untrusted node) can decrypt. For \"private from a specific member,\" use per-recipient (ECDH) encryption — a separate mechanism, deferred. Option for stronger isolation: put untrusted parties on a separate mesh key and bridge.")
    (para "**Now vs later:** ship membership (HMAC) + the message bus first; the `(key-id)`/`(sig)` slots are reserved in message-format.sxp so signing drops in without a schema break. Add signatures when authority-bearing messages become real (the sdts/trading path).")))
